# Defect 073, repaired 2026-09-20 at M-declared-extents step 19.
#
# SYMPTOM. `owned <freer>` on a parameter the caller hands IN was `check` exit
# **0** and `build` exit **2**, `internal error: compiling the generated C
# failed`, with clang holding out an optional's struct where the header wants a
# `const char *`. The compiler blaming itself for a binding the author wrote,
# which is the failure design.md §4.19's guarantee exists to prevent.
#
# CAUSE, and it is the third time this one guard has been found watching the
# wrong half. `spec § 13` has always said `owned` belongs *"after a `cstr`
# result or a `char **` out-parameter"*, and `wrong_owned_type` asks the TYPE.
# A by-value `cstr` is a `cstr`, so it walks straight past — and then
# `handed_back_param` retypes the parameter to `str?` anyway, which is why the
# argument below has to be an `ok(...)` to reach `check` at all.
#
# The guard's own comment records the two earlier rounds of exactly this:
# `unknown_freer` asked whether the NAME resolves and nothing asked what the
# mark was ON (2026-09-13), and then it asked the PARAMETER and not the RESULT
# (2026-09-15, defect 036). Each time the note beside it already named the rule
# the guard was not asking.
#
# THE SAME DIAGNOSTIC CODE and not a sibling, because it is one question — does
# this mark say anything the compiler can act on? — and `check/lending.hero`'s
# reasoning at defect 067 is that a second name for one question is a panel
# path. The code's name records the half it was written for; its note has always
# covered both.
#
# WHAT STILL PASSES, and it is the cheapest evidence the repair is narrow: every
# `owned` parameter in this repository is already a cell.
# `examples/ledger/db/sqlite.hero` writes `@error: cstr owned sqlite3_free` and
# `tests/golden/run/ffi-owned-cell-is-freed.hero` writes `@out: cstr owned free`.
# Both check at exit 0 after this repair.

extern "stdlib.h"
    function free(p: ptr)

    #~v owned_needs_cstr
    function atoi(s: cstr owned free) -> i32

function main()
    print(to_str(atoi(s: ok("12"))))
